providerResponses.text:
{
  "decision": "REVISE",
  "detail": {
    "days": 14,
    "checkpoint": "Independently solve and explain one changed simple series circuit."
  },
  "plans": [
    {
      "planRef": "plan_1",
      "blueprint": {
        "schemaVersion": 2,
        "strategy": {
          "policy": "EXAM",
          "outcome": "Explain and use Ohm’s law for one simple series circuit. Stop once I can solve and explain one changed circuit independently.",
          "explanation": "No verified assessment weighting or deadline is supplied, so the accepted performance outcome itself is the only exam evidence: solving and explaining one changed simple series circuit independently. Because no readiness evidence exists, the single integrated task supplies the minimal constructs (V = I R, same current in series, resistances add) and a worked example, then requires independent performance. A separate practice set or repeated check would add no distinct evidence for this bounded outcome.",
          "recommendedEffort": {
            "lowMinutes": 30,
            "likelyMinutes": 45,
            "highMinutes": 60
          },
          "phases": []
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The complete requested outcome: Ohm’s law and series-circuit reasoning applied to one changed simple series circuit."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "ohm_series_task",
            "outcomeRefs": [],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1"
            ],
            "title": "Learn, solve and explain one changed Ohm's law series circuit",
            "activityType": "Integrated guided-to-independent problem task",
            "purpose": "Introduce the minimal Ohm’s law and series-circuit constructs, then produce independent evidence by solving and explaining one changed simple series circuit.",
            "instructions": "Review the two rules you will use: Ohm’s law, V = I × R (the voltage across a component equals the current through it multiplied by its resistance); and for a simple series circuit, the same current flows through every component and the total resistance is the sum of the individual resistances.\n\nWork through this supplied example to see the method. A 12 V battery is connected to a 3 Ω resistor and a 6 Ω resistor in series. Total resistance R_total = 3 Ω + 6 Ω = 9 Ω. Current I = V / R_total = 12 V / 9 Ω ≈ 1.33 A. Voltage across the 3 Ω resistor: V_3 = I × 3 Ω ≈ 4.0 V. Voltage across the 6 Ω resistor: V_6 = I × 6 Ω ≈ 8.0 V. Check: 4.0 V + 8.0 V = 12 V, and each voltage satisfies V = I × R with the current you found.\n\nNow choose one changed simple series circuit with different numerical values. Do not reuse the example numbers. For example, you could use a 9 V battery with a 2 Ω resistor and a 4 Ω resistor, or any two different resistors and a different battery voltage.\n\nFor your changed circuit, write down the given voltage and resistance values. Compute the total resistance. Compute the current. Compute the voltage across each resistor. Show your equations and include units.\n\nCheck your answers: the two resistor voltages should add to the battery voltage, and each resistor’s voltage should satisfy V = I × R with the current you found.\n\nFinally, explain in your own words why the current is the same through both resistors in a series circuit, and how you used Ohm’s law to find each voltage.\n\nWork independently on your changed circuit. Do not ask for help with it. If you get stuck, re-read the rules and your own equations. Stop when you have a correct solution and explanation for your changed circuit.",
            "completionCriterion": "You independently solve one changed simple series circuit by correctly computing total resistance, current, and each resistor voltage, and explain the reasoning using Ohm’s law and series rules without assistance.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 30,
              "likelyMinutes": 45,
              "highMinutes": 60
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 60,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": []
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [
            "No prior readiness evidence is available, so the task explicitly introduces V = I R and series rules rather than assuming they are already known."
          ],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "The single integrated task includes the minimal construct explanation, a supplied worked example, and one independent changed-circuit performance. A separate practice set or second check is omitted because it would not add distinct evidence for this bounded outcome and would extend past the requested stopping point.",
            "The 60-minute timebox is longer than the preferred 30-minute chunk because the independent changed-circuit solution and explanation need one continuous run; daily capacity is 480 minutes with no hard availability conflict, so no split is required."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "Whether the learner already knows V = I R or series rules is unknown; the activity supplies them so the outcome does not depend on that prior knowledge."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "Stop once the learner has independently solved and explained one changed simple series circuit; no further practice or checks are required."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "sharedDependencies": [],
  "tradeOffExplanation": "No verified assessment weighting or deadline is supplied, so the plan treats independent performance on one changed simple series circuit as the only accepted exam evidence. The single 60-minute integrated task embeds the necessary construct instruction and worked example, then requires independent solution and explanation. A separate practice set, repeated checks, or method-selection drill is omitted because it adds no distinct evidence for this bounded outcome and would extend past the requested stopping point. The timebox is longer than the preferred 30-minute chunk because the independent performance needs one continuous run; daily capacity is 480 minutes with no hard conflict, so no split is required."
}

